Module 1: Basic C++ Syntax & Program Structure  
1.1 Anatomy of a C++ Program
#include <iostream>      // Preprocessor directive
using namespace std;     // Namespace declaration

int main() {              // Entry point — execution always starts here
  cout << "Hello, DSA!" << endl;   // Statement — ends with a semicolon
    return 0;              // Exit status: 0 = success
}

1.2 Line-by-line breakdown
Element
Explanation
#include <iostream>
Preprocessor directive — pulls in the input/output library. Runs before compilation.
using namespace std;
Lets us write cout instead of std::cout.
int main()
Every C++ program needs exactly one main(). Return type int reports exit status to the OS.
{ }
Curly braces define a block — a group of statements.
;
Every statement ends with a semicolon — the #1 beginner compile error is forgetting this.
//
Single-line comment. /* ... */ for multi-line.
return 0;
Ends main(), 0 = program ran successfully.

1.3 Compilation process (brief, conceptual)
Source Code (.cpp) → Preprocessor → Compiler → Linker → Executable (.exe)

Preprocessor handles #include, #define before actual compilation.
Compiler translates C++ to machine code, catches syntax errors.
Linker combines your code with library code (like iostream's implementation).
1.4 Identifiers, Keywords, and Case Sensitivity
int age = 21;        // 'age' is an identifier, 'int' is a keyword
int Age = 22;         // Different variable! C++ is case-sensitive

Rules for identifiers:
Can contain letters, digits, underscore — must not start with a digit.
Cannot be a reserved keyword (int, if, for, class, etc.)
Convention: camelCase or snake_case for variables, meaningful names (totalMarks, not x)
1.5 Whitespace & Indentation
C++ ignores extra whitespace/line breaks — but always indent consistently. It's not enforced by the compiler, but unreadable code becomes a real problem once DSA logic gets nested (loops inside loops inside conditions).



